{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1562143-v5-project-rules-how-do-you-use-them","id":1562143,"title":"[V5] Project Rules - how do you use them?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-07-25T08:20:19-07:00","id":1562143,"title":"[V5] Project Rules - how do you use them?","content":"I've read both the core book Project Rules and the Player's Guide version, and I really want to know how others engage (or not) with those systems.<br /> <br /> Projects rules at first seem like an interesting concept: how to execute long-term initiatives by the players whose benefits are tangible and can be objectively defined.<br /> <br /> But I found them badly written and excessively punishing, at least until you manage to reliably get 12+ dice pools, when it's maybe not hard enough. I don't think they're too convoluted, but they certainly could use better wording.<br /> <br /> And the Player's Guide version basically throws everything away with what boils down to a way of describing regular Extended Actions, but easier to perform and with wonky wording. By the way, overall I get from that book that the writer had way less understanding of the basic rules than they should, even if the ideas within are sound.<br /> <br /> Making better rules shouldn't be hard, but first I'd like to know from the community: do you use Projects at all? How? What changes if any you make and why? What's your opinion on the RAW for them?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T10:10:41-07:00","id":1562149,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In all the times I've played V5, Project rules never came up. I'll be honest, I just finished reading them; they are so well hidden I wasn't even aware they existed, lol.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don't have access to the Player's Guide, so I'll comment on the Core Book rules.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Outside of the initial roll, I don't find them particularly punishing, as long as you don't try to expedite the project. It seems to me like the project advances on its own at each increment after the initial launch test.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Goal Rolls seem broken to me. I don't like that they can finish the project in one attempt. You can technically launch a millennia-long project, get a lucky roll, and have it end the week after. I also don't like that a non-thinking entity rolls a pool. I'd rather have a fixed difficulty, unless it's a very chaotic and unpredictable project.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I also don't really like rolling Background + Skill, but I know it's something V5 uses elsewhere. In my opinion, Backgrounds are tools, not skill sets.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I also dislike the gambling aspect of it. You can gamble Backgrounds, which are worth experience points, and lose them. It's been a while since I've played V5, but as far as i remember there's no sanctity of merits, so those are just lost experience points. Since you can't spend Willpower, you're only one unlucky roll away from losing all your stake at the project launch, this feels bads and is completely outside the player's control.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don't know what I would change personally. I find the rules simple and yet weirdly unwieldy. Nothing that an extended test or a social maneuver system like CoD couldn't handle on its own.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Generally, in my current CoD campaign, I ask player to accumulate a number of successes to reach their goal and if they have background dot they can spend, such as resources, contact, allies, etc, I let them add them as automatic success toward their goal.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In my non-vampire homebrew system, the way I handle project is that it has a base difficulty and a completion threshold that\u2019s a fixed number of successes. If you beat the base difficulty when you make a test, you add 1 + the margin of success toward the completion. There are other technicalities for handle complications and failures, but it essentially boils down to this.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Another way to project would probably be to assign it &quot;Doors&quot; and, at each given increment, ask how the player wants to proceed, then set a difficulty based on what they are trying to achieve and how it relates to their project. On a win, they'd open a Door, or two on a critical win. If they wanted to expedite things, maybe I'd add a penalty based on the number of extra Doors they wanted to open.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I wouldn't let a single die roll complete a project; otherwise, it could have been solved in a scene.</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T11:10:28-07:00","id":1562151,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1562143-v5-project-rules-how-do-you-use-them?p=1562149#post1562149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Goal Rolls seem broken to me. I don't like that they can finish the project in one attempt. You can technically launch a millennia-long project, get a lucky roll, and have it end the week after. I also don't like that a non-thinking entity rolls a pool. I'd rather have a fixed difficulty, unless it's a very chaotic and unpredictable project.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree the Goals have this problem. The ST still defines when the benefit comes within the Increment, so a millennia long project could still require at least 100 years, and for what you're saying to happen, it would mean the PC rolled against a 10 dice Pool and managed to get 10 successes <i><b>more</b></i> than it. That's 15 successes in average in a roll you have to get every success without criticals or bonuses.<br /> <br /> The nonthinking entity is my Difficulty problem: the need to beat a Pool of 10 dice. In fact, this Difficulty is flat for all Projects, no matter the Scope or your planning. You're facing a 10-dice opposition and evolves from there. It's like a 10 success fixed Extended Action that damages your Backgrounds forever if you don't roll very well.<br /> <br /> Also the Initiation roll is too necessary. It says it should be reserved for exceptional Projects, but as written it's your only way of getting a roll that scales with Scope to try and make the whole endeavor less punishing.<br /> <br /> With a table for bonuses that let you adjust the whole thing it could work, but as a flat challenge rolling 10 dice with advantage, I'm really wary of it. And I agree that Doors would be a better mechanic here anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T13:41:06-07:00","id":1562153,"title":"","content":"I have a few examples of things I'm wanting to do in the chronicle I'm currently playing. How would you run those in the current system? Below each one is my personal take (not the ST and using the core rules).<br /> <br /> For reference, the character is close to start, with 26 XP total (15 initial). 4 dots in Backgrounds, 3 of them being her Haven. I guess if I bet them I'm homeless? No, just the security systems stop working for reasons.<br /> <br /> 1. I'm trying to seed some goodwill by catering to the vanity of the local Regent (she's a Prince for half the city). I'm not set in a benefit, yet. It's a simple idea for now, I'm carving a netsuke of her as one of the Seven Fortune Gods of Japan (Shichifukujin) (my character is Japanese, but the chronicle is set up in Virginia). My Crafts is 2, not a main skill, just a hobby, rolls will prob be ~4 dice.<br /> <br /> Netsuke \u2013 Scope 1 \u2013 Increment (Night) \u2013 Initiation Diff 3 \u2013 Bet: 2 dots \u2013 Die 10<br /> <br /> 2. One of the PCs is the owner of an Arcade, and I'm a game developer. To start to increase my reputation in the local scene and expand the scene itself, I'm going to make an indie game for him in my style (the character was main dev of Pokemon in the chronicle). For Benefit I thought maybe Fame within a Mask to start, which would give me 2 Merit dots. My Pool for this one is 10, though I can use legal bonuses for Projects that get to 12.<br /> <br /> Arcade Game \u2013 Scope 2 \u2013 Increment (Night) \u2013 Initiation Diff 4 \u2013 Bet: 3 dots \u2013 Die 10<br /> <br /> 3. My most ambitious Project for now is to find an Ujigami for the city. The character is Shinto, so she believes every place has a kami even if locals are unaware. She will try to identify candidates, locate, select, start contact, propitiate, start a small shrine and establish a proper cult. It's the first that look like a proper Project.<br /> <br /> Ujigami \u2013 Scope 3 \u2013 Increment (weekly) \u2013 initiation Diff 5 \u2013 Bet: 4 dots \u2013 Die 10<br /> <br /> 4. I'm also working in a dedicated chat app for the group, enhancing safety. I'm not sure how to represent this in game terms, but O think it's probably worth 1 or 2 dots.<br /> <br /> NightChat \u2013 Scope 1/2 \u2013 Increment (Night) \u2013 Initiation Diff 3/4 \u2013 Bet: 2/3 dots \u2013 Die 10<br /> <br /> 5. My actual occult related idea. The character cooks Japanese confectionery as a way to stay connected to her country, prefers Ningy\u014dyaki, a recipe from her home in Asakusa, T\u014dky\u014d. She's not a stellar cook, but she's invested. One of her recipes for fillings includes Blood in the fillings, but the cooking process destroys any supernatural effect. She'll research a way to make a recipe that survives the process and stay effective in the final product. Let's say this is strong enough to warrant 4 dots.<br /> <br /> Kaoru K\u014dhi \u2013 Scope 4 \u2013 Increment (monthly) \u2013 Initiation Diff 6 \u2013 Bet 5 dots \u2013 Die 10<br /> <br /> <br /> I'm aware not all of those are good fits for the Project rules, but I'd like to know how you'd adapt them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T14:41:15-07:00","id":1562157,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1562143-v5-project-rules-how-do-you-use-them?p=1562151#post1562151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I disagree the Goals have this problem. The ST still defines when the benefit comes within the Increment, so a millennia long project could still require at least 100 years, and for what you're saying to happen, it would mean the PC rolled against a 10 dice Pool and managed to get 10 successes <i><b>more</b></i> than it.<br /> That's 15 successes in average in a roll you have to get every success without criticals or bonuses.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Aah you\u2019re right, I must have glanced over that part, my bad. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Indeed, then it simply means that the goal test is needlessly punishing and hardcore.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">A pool using Skill + Background won\u2019t generally be that high. Accounting for lack of crits I don\u2019t think I would personally attempt to roll before I have at least 3 dice above the project die pool and even then, losing background on a bad roll would <i>suck hard. </i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1562143-v5-project-rules-how-do-you-use-them?p=1562151#post1562151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The nonthinking entity is my Difficulty problem: the need to beat a Pool of 10 dice. In fact, this Difficulty is flat for all Projects, no matter the Scope or your planning. You're facing a 10-dice opposition and evolves from there. It's like a 10 success fixed Extended Action that damages your Backgrounds forever if you don't roll very well.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Yeah, the rule is not great it lacks flexibility and is weirdly punishing.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As for your question and examples; do you ask how I would run it with the Project rules as close to RAW as possible, with lose interpretation, or with any other system at all?</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T14:51:24-07:00","id":1562159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1562143-v5-project-rules-how-do-you-use-them?p=1562157#post1562157\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Aah you\u2019re right, I must have glanced over that part, my bad. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Indeed, then it simply means that the goal test is needlessly punishing and hardcore.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">A pool using Skill + Background won\u2019t generally be that high. Accounting for lack of crits I don\u2019t think I would personally attempt to roll before I have at least 3 dice above the project die pool and even then, losing background on a bad roll would <i>suck hard. </i></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Yeah, the rule is not great it lacks flexibility and is weirdly punishing.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As for your question and examples; do you ask how I would run it with the Project rules as close to RAW as possible, with lose interpretation, or with any other system at all?</span></div> </div> </div> </div>All you care to share. Close to raw would help to see ways to use it, but your personal takes would help design alternatives.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T18:01:06-07:00","id":1562166,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Alrighty, then let\u2019s start with RAW.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019ll restrict myself to the core book, since it\u2019s all I have access and try to run each example with these three stated goal in mind:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I want to use the rule as written</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I want the character to be able to at least start their project. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019ll engage in good faith, to strongman the rules.</span></li> </ul><hr /> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Example 1 - Netsuke</b></span></div> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">You already gave all the necessary details for the project, so first we need to settle on a dice pool for the launch test, which will be determined by your approach. Since the Regent is a Kindred, discipline uses are prohibited in the launch roll. We have to use background + Skill.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Any of the following would be appropriate:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Haven + Craft</b> to prepare to set your workshop. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Resource + Occult </b>to find interesting materials with obscure spiritual meaning. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Contact + Academic </b>to find interesting lore about netsuke, and materials native to the state of Virginia in an attempt to craft something that both represents your origin and destination. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Insight + Status </b>to find information about what the regent would prefer for material, by talking to people from their entourage. </span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Normally you would have to stake at least 1 dot for the benefit, but it\u2019s not required. Let\u2019s say the Benefit is one dot Mawla. The Regent is certainly powerful, but they won\u2019t devolve their whole attention to you after one gift.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Since the scope is 1, you decide to bet 2 dots of haven a bit of a stretch given the project, but we end up justifying that you are making a place for your workshop, you are spending increased time in your haven, running the risk of attracting more hunter around your safe place or other court member starts to investigate your increased absence. Two dots of havens are lost as a result of installing your workshop. (I know a bit intense for a Netsuke, but let\u2019s say you have a <i>very messy </i>workshop).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Let\u2019s assume you go with Contact + Academic and the launch roll succeeds, we roleplay a scene where you go around antique Japanese shop to find inspiration, look online for inspiration, etc. You eventually set up for Cornus Florida(Flowering dogwood) as it\u2019s Virginia state tree.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Once that\u2019s done, we set up the Project die to 10. Since you only have 2 dots of haven and 2 dots of craft, we are looking at a pool of 4 dice. Rolling before the Project die reaches down to 3 is a very risky endeavor so you opt not to roll.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Once the project die reaches 3, you realize that you won\u2019t make it to Elysium in time if you don\u2019t start working faster, so you decide to roll Haven + Craft.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">You roll a pool of 4, I roll a pool of 3. You win by a margin of 2. Now only 1 die remains. Since the Elysium is in 2 days.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Conclusion:</b> You Successfully complete your gift in time. The Regent will be touched with your gift. We roleplay a scene at the Elysium where your character gift the Netsuke to the Regent, who\u2019s touched by your thoughtfulness. You gain a dot of Mawla, which I award for free for choosing to risk your background.</span><br /> <hr /> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Example 2 \u2013 Arcade Game</b></span></div> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m not sure how your character reaches 12 in the dice pool(I also can\u2019t find legal bonuses, but I\u2019ll assume you have it, no worries). However, without more detail it\u2019s difficult a bit to determine how I would run it, as I don\u2019t know what the pool constitutes.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Since you have a pool of 12 however, we can assume you\u2019ll ace the launch test. Since you\u2019re looking for fame, the launch should probably involve an actual online announcement to drive hype for your upcoming game.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">With 12 dice pool, your game can probably be safely completed within 4 nights. As once the project dice rolls 6, you can start rolling at anytime to reduce the project dice. (It doesn\u2019t seem like there\u2019s a time limit between each attempted rolls).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Since each roll is supposed to represent a dramatic shift maybe your character use Haven + Survival, stays awake during the day to time crunch, or they hire some extra help.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Benefits:</b> Influence in the gaming industry might also be appropriate for benefits. Contacts or retainers also seems appropriate.</span><br /> <hr /> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Conclusion on Raw</b></span></div> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Not going to lie, I think I have hit a wall.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I was about to engage with the other examples, but I find the system frustrating in some aspect.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Your projects are interesting, but running raw with the limitations of skill + background is incredibly limiting, I <i>really dislike</i> that rule. Not all characters have backgrounds and forcing interpretation on how to use certain background feels <i>weird. </i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The goal roll is also incredibly weird: you also somehow have to wait for the project die to decrease on its own before you can attempt to roll, then you can try to rush in the end to catch up on missed time. This feels weird.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Also, since the goal test is so hard, it pushes the player to always seek the most optimal dice pool, while I feel like a project often involves multiple skills. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Your listed examples seem fine, but since it all involves a single dice pool, it feels like there\u2019s not much to work there(Not your fault, the fault of the Project rules). I would want at least a little bit more interaction in a project, or creative choice to better reflect the decisions that come with running a complex project.</span><br /> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>How I would run the examples</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">First, I should state that I would not award free dots for projects nor would I allow a player to risk their dot without the sanctity of merits. I would simply award experience as I would with any other successfully completed plot kit or story beat. If the player took part in a project that\u2019s excuse to gain the background dot they were interested in gaining. That\u2019s not really too relevant, however.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">With your given example, I think a social maneuver system, re-fluffed for the project would work best. This is what i would recommend. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Or if you wanted to be a bit more involved in the project, you could outline each step, working with the player to find how they want to engage with their project and bring it to life. Maybe I\u2019d leave some space opened for extra complications, or, etc. As you'll see bellow, this is a bit more involved however. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">If we wanted a basic number, maybe I\u2019d go with 1 + twice the amount of background dots gained. I wouldn\u2019t necessarily set a fixed time; I would instead base it on what would feel right for what\u2019s been asked and the method used.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"> I would set a maximum number of failures per steps, probably 2, or award an increased penalty based on the number of failed tests, the test would become impossible if the penalty reached 3.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Taking some of your examples, here's how it could work</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Example 1 \u2013 Netsuke.</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">You want to craft a netsuke for the local Regent to gain 1 dot of Mawla. This would be a 3-step project. Let\u2019s outline the steps:</span><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Material:</b> You need to find the right material that will impress the Regent and where you can find it. I determine that it\u2019s easy enough, you need a successful Academic + Intelligence test at difficulty 2. Let\u2019s say this takes a night. </span> <ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Critical win:</b> You\u2019re very inspired or find some very good material, gain +1 on the crafting test.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On a win :</b> You acquire the material. Remove your project penalty.</span></li> </ol></li> </ol><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On a failure:</b> You need to spend 2 dot resources, or you can\u2019t find what you are looking for. If you don\u2019t spend resources, your project penalty increases by 1.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Total Failure:</b> Your project penalty increases by 2.</span></li> </ol></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Crafting. </b>You need to craft the items. A netsuke is a small item, so I\u2019ll ask for Dexterity + Craft. At <i>your chosen </i>difficulty, maximum 6. This takes 3 nights. </span> <ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Critical win:</b> Incredible, you\u2019ll have +1 to impress the regent.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Win:</b> You successfully crafted an item. This takes 3 nights. Remove your project penalty.</span></li> </ol></li> </ol><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On a failure:</b> You are wasting precious materials. You wasted 1 night instead of 3. Your project penalty increases by 1. </span></li> </ol></li> </ol><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Total Failure:</b> You wasted 3 nights and you are nearly out of material. Your project penalty increases by 2.</span></li> </ol></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Gifting . </b>The most stressful part. You want to impress the regent, what if they simply refuse your gift? Roll Charisma + Etiquette to impress the regent while you over them your gift at difficulty 9 - original your crafting difficulty. This is a simple meeting and takes nothing more than a few minutes at Elysium or at her office. </span> <ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Critical Win:</b> The regent is very impressed; she makes a comment about how the Night society would stand to have more kindred like you. You gain +1 on your next interactions at Elysium and you can buy the Mawla background at 1 dot.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Win: </b>The Regent is touched. They tell you that if you need anything come and ask, she\u2019ll see what she can do. You gain a Mawla at 1 dot.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Failure:</b> The Regent doesn\u2019t understand the significance of your gift. You may try to explain yourself using another skill or use Ettiquette again. Your project penalty increases by 1.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Total Failure:</b> The Regent misinterpret your intentions; you only have one more chance to make it right. Your project penalty increases by 2.</span></li> </ol></li> </ol><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Example 3 \u2013 Ujigami.</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">You want to build a cult centered around an Ujigami. For the sake of this project, I will assume that no actual spirit is actually involved, but that it centered around building a cult, simply because I do not know what it could represent in masquerade term.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">This will grant you 1 dot of contacts, 1 dot of influence and 2 dots retainer as you gain a devoted cult follower. Since this is 4 dots project, this is a 9-step project. This is a lot of steps, so I will group some of them.</span><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Finding the Ujigami \u2013 Step 1 to 2: </b>During this step, you investigate the city; looking at how a spirit from Virginia could manifest. I\u2019ll ask for resolve + Occult at difficulty 3 to keep your mind open despite the different land. Each test represents a week of investigation. </span> <ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Critical Win:</b> You complete steps 1 and 2. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Win:</b> Progress by one step. If your complete step 2, remove your project penalty.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Failure:</b> You suffer a setback. You can roll again but increase your penalty by 1. If your penalty reaches 3 the project fails.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Total Failure:</b> You suffer a serious setback, increase your penalty by 2. If your penalty reaches 3 the project fails.</span></li> </ol></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Recruiting for the cult \u2013 Step 3 to 5: </b>Building the cult represents recruiting people that open to your beliefs. I\u2019ll ask for a manipulation + Leadership or Wits + Insight. Each test takes 1 week of networking.</span> <ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Critical Win: </b>Count as one extra step, up to step 5.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Win: </b>Progress one step. If you complete step 5 removes all project penalty.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Failure: </b>You suffer a setback, increase your project penalty by 1.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Failure: </b>You suffer a serious setback, some people are starting to be weary of your insistence to recruit them into your cult, increase your project penalty by 2.</span></li> </ol></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Building the Shrine \u2013 Step 6 to 8: </b>Starting the Shrine involves finding the right place for worship or simply building it. Roll either Intelligence + Craft or Resolve + Investigation at difficulty 4.</span> <ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Critical Win: </b>Count as one extra step, up to step 8.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Win: </b>Progress one step. If you complete step 5 removes all project penalty.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Failure: </b>You suffer a setback, increase your project penalty by 1.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Failure: </b>You suffer a serious setback, some people are starting to be weary of your insistence to recruit them into your cult, increase your project penalty by 2.</span></li> </ol></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Gaining your cult follower\u2013 Step 9: </b>You need to find the right person. One that either believes in your cult or will be devoted enough to do what you ask of them. Roll Wits + Insight against difficulty 5 to find the right person, or Charisma + Leadership to motivate someone to step up. Each test takes a week.</span> <ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Critical Win:</b> You find your devotee they are perfect. Heal a point of willpower damage as you find newfound confidence in your endeavor. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Win:</b> You complete your project, your cult is built, you can now worship far away from your home.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Failure:</b> No one is the right fit, hopefully you can find someone during the next ceremony. Increase your project penalty by 1.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Total failure: </b>You are reminded that you are no longer home and you struggle to connect. Increase your project penalty by 2</span></li> </ol></li> </ol><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Conclusion</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I realized after having typed it that the second project is quite long. I don\u2019t think the storyteller needs to plan everything in advance, but maybe dividing the project in multiple simpler project might work better if steps are going to be more free from.<br /> <br /> It would also benefit from serious rules for win, failure, total failure and critical win. That would require a lot more effort in designing it. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Maybe limiting scope to a single dot per extended test might be better, I\u2019m not sure.<br /> <br /> Hope this was useful in any way, but i think your original assessment of the Project Rules are correct. </span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T00:14:09-07:00","id":1562174,"title":"","content":"I like your version, and agree with your observations.<br /> <br /> To be honest, the rules for Extended Actions in V5 are by themselves better Project rules than the Project rules. Regular Extended add to a final result like normal. Serial Extended works as a fixed succession of tests you must beat in order, very similar to your take. Hard Extended fuse both, you make independent rolls and at the same time the extra successes add to a final goal. And Cascading Extended is like Hard but instead of adding to a final goal, the extra successes work as bonus dice on the following roll.<br /> <br /> And then we get to the end of the book and the Project Rules are what they are...<br /> <br /> Player's Guide version is just a Regular Extended Action with 3-6 successes as goal, which is actually quite low.<br /> <br /> Oh, on my bonuses: I have High-Function Addict (stimulants) and Laboratory in my Haven, each gives a +1 dice bonus and are not in the exclusion clauses of Projects. I have a 10 dice Pool for coding thanks to Specialization and usually this is Intelligence. With Int 4 + Technology 5 + Specialization it's 10 dice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-27T16:12:54-07:00","id":1562241,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Thanks! <span style=\"font-family:Segoe UI Emoji\">\ud83d\ude0a</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Yeah, you\u2019re 100% right, having just reread the extended test just now, it\u2019s close to what I had in mind when I wrote the second examples.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I haven\u2019t run v5 in for a while, but in my homebrew, system extended test, which can handle simply crafting and investigation are very close to \u2018\u2019Hard extended test\u2019\u2019 from V5. I find that extended tests work best when time is a factor, otherwise, I prefer a simple roll or series of tests to achieve a goal.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The Project rules are both weirdly restrictive, yet bare bones. So I felt stuck between worst of both worlds while attempting to engage with them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As for your character's pool, i see, your character is incredibly skilled! II think RAW, it should be background + Skill, so I'm not sure if you\u2019d get to 10 as the pool thus intelligence 4 wouldn\u2019t, but since you do have 5 in technology so your character should be good with using the project rule nonetheless.</span><br /> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">When I run an improvised investigation in my current Tokyo by Night VTR chronicle, since successes are a bit rarer (8+), I\u2019ll usually announce to the group that they need to hit a number of successes to clear the investigation. The only difference with true extended test is that I don\u2019t limit the number of tests to attribute + skill pool, since only time is a factor. So I\u2019ll generally ask for a bit more success usually 10 to 20, but I also convert merit dots into straight success if they make sense. We\u2019ll do a little montage where I ask everyone how they contribute, maybe run some micro scenes or two, and I\u2019ll reveal basic information for each 1/3 of the necessary, as much as it makes sense, until they complete the investigation and find out what they want.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">For example, last game player was trying to find a financial fraud investigator\u2019s house so they could blackmail him into leaving the poor millionaire kindred of the coterie alone. I asked players to accumulate 15 successes and how they wanted to proceed:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The millionaire being a hacker tried to find information about his online trace to track him back to his home. Rolled intelligence + Computer, rolled something like 4 successes.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">One player had contacts &amp; allies within the Yakuza, used something like 4 dots of merits + rolled a socialize + manipulation to asks where her \u2018\u2019uncle\u2019\u2019 office was near the tax agency headquarters. Rolled something like 2 successes, for a total of +6. She learned the area in which the guy lived, they were only missing his house. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Once they had an area, the Mekhet investigated using Auspex 1 to find the most likely house for their target. I allowed her to use her auspex pool at the cost of a rouse check. She managed to clear the investigation.</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Unfortunately for them, since they moved a lot of influence, quickly without being super discreet about it, I diced to roll to see if the agent could notice their investigation, I rolled something like 7 successes for the bad ass agent who was used to this kind of shenanigans having investigated of lot Takahashi fraud in the past, so he managed to leave his house and take a flight outside the country before they could find him.</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}